=== HINT 1: Register handler ===
PVOID handler = AddVectoredExceptionHandler(1, div_zero_handler);

=== HINT 2: Inside div_zero_handler ===
The faulting instruction on x64 is typically:
  idiv rcx   (or edx:eax / ecx)
To continue safely, set the divisor register to 1 and let it retry:
  ep->ContextRecord->Rcx = 1;
  return EXCEPTION_CONTINUE_EXECUTION;

Or set the result register(s) to 0 and skip the instruction:
  ep->ContextRecord->Rax = 0;
  ep->ContextRecord->Rdx = 0;
  ep->ContextRecord->Rip += 2;   // skip the 2-byte `div rcx` opcode (FRAGILE)

The cleanest approach: just set Rcx=1 and CONTINUE_EXECUTION.

=== HINT 3: Remove handler ===
RemoveVectoredExceptionHandler(handler);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
VEH fires BEFORE the stack is unwound (unlike SEH).
This means you see every exception even inside try blocks.
Anti-debug: if STATUS_BREAKPOINT fires without you setting a BP, a debugger is attached.
Instrumentation tools (Dr. Memory, DynamoRIO) use VEH to intercept every fault.

VEH order: VEH handlers -> SEH frame handlers -> UnhandledExceptionFilter -> crash.
